3
00:00:19,759 --> 00:00:23,783
I do not say happy, though I'm sure it has been a happy one for you.

4
00:00:24,824 --> 00:00:32,493
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhassa.

5
00:00:33,293 --> 00:00:41,243
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhassa.

6
00:00:41,914 --> 00:00:56,567
Namo tassa bhagavato avahato Sambha sambuddhasam Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

7
00:00:56,588 --> 00:01:06,578
So this evening we're looking at the Samagama Sutta discourse.

8
00:01:06,597 --> 00:01:09,120
And this is

9
00:01:10,112 --> 00:01:17,025
Concerning the Jains again, so this is what happened according to the Buddhist propaganda, you might say.

10
00:01:17,045 --> 00:01:23,536
I don't know whether this happened or not, but definitely something happened when the Nagantha, the leader of the Jains, died.

11
00:01:24,115 --> 00:01:25,319
And this is what is said.

12
00:01:26,721 --> 00:01:36,356
On his death, the Naganthas divided, split into two, and they had taken to quarreling and brawling and were deep in dispute, stabbing each other with verbal daggers.

13
00:01:37,129 --> 00:01:39,472
You do not understand this Dharma and discipline.

14
00:01:39,894 --> 00:01:41,516
I understand this Dharma and discipline.

15
00:01:41,756 --> 00:01:44,239
How could you understand this Dharma and discipline?

16
00:01:44,659 --> 00:01:45,480
Your way is wrong.

17
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:46,421
My way is right.

18
00:01:46,763 --> 00:01:47,804
I am consistent.

19
00:01:47,823 --> 00:01:48,924
You are inconsistent.

20
00:01:48,965 --> 00:01:51,328
What should have been said first, you said last.

21
00:01:51,388 --> 00:01:53,370
What should have been said last, you said first.

22
00:01:54,052 --> 00:01:58,037
What you had so carefully thought up has been turned inside out.

23
00:01:58,498 --> 00:02:01,441
Your assertion has been blown up, shown up, excuse me.

24
00:02:01,621 --> 00:02:02,563
You are refuted.

25
00:02:03,024 --> 00:02:07,069
Go and learn better or disentangle yourself if you can.

26
00:02:07,537 --> 00:02:31,469
hmm daggers definitely so it seems there was nothing but slaughter amongst the negantha's pupils i think that's a bit of an older statement i don't think you mean it don't mean it physically and the white clothed lay disciples were disgusted dismayed and disappointed with the negantha's pupils as they were with so badly proclaimed and badly expounded dharma and discipline

27
00:02:31,770 --> 00:02:42,866
which was un-emancipating, unconducive to peace, expounded by one not fully enlightened, and was now with its shrine broken, left without a refuge.

28
00:02:43,546 --> 00:02:45,629
So that's a bit of Buddhist propaganda, I think.

29
00:02:47,552 --> 00:02:58,587
Anyway, the novice Chunda, who was seemingly a younger brother of Sariputta, was spending the rains where Indiganta supposedly died in Parva.

30
00:03:00,086 --> 00:03:06,418
And he met Ananda and he told Ananda about all these goings on.

31
00:03:06,478 --> 00:03:11,687
And Ananda said, well, we'd better go and have a chat with the Buddha, with the Blessed One.

32
00:03:12,769 --> 00:03:17,195
So they tell the Blessed One, the Buddha, everything that's been happening.

33
00:03:18,037 --> 00:03:20,020
And the Buddha replies, what do you think, Ananda?

34
00:03:20,622 --> 00:03:24,829
These things that I have taught you directly, in other words, my teachings,

35
00:03:25,652 --> 00:03:47,776
That is, and these are the 37 factors that lead to liberation, the Bodhi Pakiya Dhamma, the four foundations of mindfulness, the four right kinds of striving, the four bases of spiritual power, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven facts of enlightenment, the Noble Eightfold Path.

36
00:03:48,137 --> 00:03:49,618
I'm sure you understand all those.

37
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:53,483
Do you see even two bhikkhus,

38
00:03:53,782 --> 00:03:56,286
who make differing assertion about these things.

39
00:03:56,306 --> 00:03:59,030
Do you see any disputes arising?

40
00:03:59,469 --> 00:04:07,741
No, Venerable Sir, I do not even see two monks or monastics differing about this.

41
00:04:08,842 --> 00:04:21,899
But Venerable Sir, there are people who live deferential towards the Blessed One who might, when he's gone, create a dispute in the Sangha about livelihood and about patimokha, the rule.

42
00:04:23,584 --> 00:04:29,653
Now, such a dispute would be for the harm and unhappiness of many, for the loss, harm and suffering of gods and humans.

43
00:04:31,435 --> 00:04:37,242
The Buddha replies, a dispute about livelihood or about patimokha in the world, livelihood, how they live.

44
00:04:39,444 --> 00:04:46,774
Remember the bhikkhus stands for all, for the four assemblies, monks, nuns, laywomen, laymen.

45
00:04:48,187 --> 00:04:51,252
So dispute about livelihood or about the patimokha.

46
00:04:51,612 --> 00:04:57,800
So the patimokha here is, of course, the specific rules, the institutional rules that govern the order.

47
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:03,747
But as lay people, you can take it as the moral law.

48
00:05:03,867 --> 00:05:06,370
Said a dispute about that would be trifling.

49
00:05:07,572 --> 00:05:15,562
But should you dispute arising the sangha about the path and the way, such a dispute would be for the harm and unhappiness of many.

50
00:05:16,031 --> 00:05:43,660
the lost harm and suffering of gods and men so he was much more concerned about the dharma now he says there are six routes to disputes and i think uh most of these we will recognize and uh you can you can bring this into your daily life where you've seen it in in the place of work amongst your friends and so on and of course in politics so someone is angry and revengeful

51
00:05:44,904 --> 00:05:55,699
and dwells disrespectful and undifferential towards the teacher, towards the Dharma, towards the Sangha, does not fulfill the training.

52
00:05:57,401 --> 00:06:10,980
Now, if we see any such root of dispute, either in ourselves or externally, you should strive to abandon the same evil root of dispute and make sure that it does not arise in the future.

53
00:06:11,382 --> 00:06:12,463
So that's the great effort.

54
00:06:12,524 --> 00:06:22,019
That's one of the great efforts, isn't it, to let go of something that's unwholesome and to stop the unwholesomeness arising.

55
00:06:22,038 --> 00:06:28,911
So the first one is this business of anger and revenge.

56
00:06:29,512 --> 00:06:31,153
Then there's contempt.

57
00:06:31,213 --> 00:06:36,562
So that anger and revenge could be, for instance, of an employee towards the employer.

58
00:06:37,014 --> 00:06:44,084
Contemptuous and domineering could be the employer towards the employee.

59
00:06:44,684 --> 00:06:50,872
Then, of course, there's always envy and avarice, wanting somebody else, wanting what other people have.

60
00:06:51,552 --> 00:06:54,675
There's deceit and fraud, fiddling.

61
00:06:55,516 --> 00:06:57,839
There's evil wishes and wrong view.

62
00:06:57,899 --> 00:07:06,009
That's basically wishing somebody that they drop dead or something.

63
00:07:07,507 --> 00:07:16,439
And there are those who adhere to their own views tenaciously and relinquishes them with difficulty, even when they're wrong.

64
00:07:16,740 --> 00:07:19,444
They won't negotiate, they won't compromise.

65
00:07:20,526 --> 00:07:24,831
So there's your six things that create the roots of dispute.

66
00:07:25,632 --> 00:07:36,367
Anger and vengefulness, contemptuous and domineering, envious and avaricious, deceitful and fraudulent,

67
00:07:36,853 --> 00:07:46,149
Evil wishes with wrong view and unable to negotiate, to come to a compromise at all.

68
00:07:46,428 --> 00:07:47,151
I'm right.

69
00:07:47,471 --> 00:07:48,593
Everybody else is wrong.

70
00:07:50,555 --> 00:07:52,439
Reminds you of the Brexit negotiations.

71
00:07:55,062 --> 00:07:57,968
So those are your six roots of dispute.

72
00:08:01,576 --> 00:08:04,158
Then he says there are four kinds of litigation.

73
00:08:04,420 --> 00:08:07,182
So this is where things have to be sorted out.

74
00:08:09,807 --> 00:08:19,538
Litigation because of dispute, litigation because of accusation, because of an offence and concerning proceedings.

75
00:08:20,019 --> 00:08:25,526
So there are four reasons why you take things to court in the order anyway.

76
00:08:26,088 --> 00:08:31,514
The first one is to try and work out the dispute, the disagreement.

77
00:08:32,034 --> 00:08:35,938
The second one is to sort out an accusation that's been made against somebody.

78
00:08:37,341 --> 00:08:39,903
Then there's one concerning a specific offence.

79
00:08:40,644 --> 00:08:42,866
And there's one about proceedings.

80
00:08:45,230 --> 00:08:49,173
And in order to sort this out, there are seven kinds of settlements.

81
00:08:49,975 --> 00:08:51,017
Okay.

82
00:08:51,037 --> 00:08:53,700
So then it becomes clear what these four things are.

83
00:08:55,461 --> 00:09:01,148
So again, this has a direct connection with ordinary daily life.

84
00:09:02,849 --> 00:09:04,772
The removal by confrontation.

85
00:09:04,871 --> 00:09:09,317
Okay, so there's a dispute, much like the Jains.

86
00:09:09,778 --> 00:09:10,720
It is the Dharma.

87
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:12,623
It is not the Dharma.

88
00:09:12,643 --> 00:09:13,604
This is the discipline.

89
00:09:13,624 --> 00:09:16,227
This is not the discipline, et cetera, et cetera.

90
00:09:16,248 --> 00:09:20,033
Now, what the Buddha says is that everybody should meet in concord.

91
00:09:20,673 --> 00:09:24,038
In other words, to come with a heart of goodwill.

92
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:29,346
And having met together, the guideline of the Dharma should be drawn out.

93
00:09:29,798 --> 00:09:33,663
In other words, they should actually discuss the way the teaching.

94
00:09:33,903 --> 00:09:37,850
So he's preparing the order for when he's not there, remember.

95
00:09:40,073 --> 00:09:45,481
And once the guideline has been drawn out, the litigation should be settled in a way which accords with it.

96
00:09:47,644 --> 00:09:52,653
Now, supposing that doesn't work, there is the opinion of the majority.

97
00:09:53,695 --> 00:09:55,317
If those bhikkhus cannot settle,

98
00:09:55,582 --> 00:10:01,337
that litigation in that dwelling place, they should go to a dwelling place where there's a greater number of bhikkhus, where there's a greater number.

99
00:10:01,818 --> 00:10:10,621
So in other words, you go to a different court, a higher court for some guidance and for the decision, okay?

100
00:10:13,841 --> 00:10:18,307
So the opinion of the majority settles the dispute.

101
00:10:18,727 --> 00:10:20,570
So there's two ways, right?

102
00:10:20,610 --> 00:10:23,693
The dispute has arisen between two groups of people.

103
00:10:24,195 --> 00:10:32,504
They try to settle it by coming together and looking at what the Dharma says, in ordinary daily life, looking at what the law says.

104
00:10:33,306 --> 00:10:37,791
And then when that doesn't work, you have to go to a bigger group or a higher court.

105
00:10:41,365 --> 00:10:44,028
Now the next three are to do with individual offences.

106
00:10:44,187 --> 00:10:47,471
So this is more like people who commit crimes.

107
00:10:49,614 --> 00:10:52,437
So the first one is a litigation on account of memory.

108
00:10:52,937 --> 00:10:56,942
So here a monk reproves another monk.

109
00:10:56,982 --> 00:11:03,028
This is more to do with monks, but as I say, you can easily transfer it into ordinary daily life.

110
00:11:03,809 --> 00:11:08,975
So somebody reproves somebody else of such a grave offence.

111
00:11:09,091 --> 00:11:13,275
In this case, it's one involving defeat or bordering on defeat.

112
00:11:13,296 --> 00:11:27,493
Remember, the rules of defeat are killing, stealing, any sexual intercourse, and creating a split in the Dharma, in the Sangha, in the Sangha, in the order of monks.

113
00:11:28,374 --> 00:11:36,903
So it would be creating hate speech in society, things like that.

114
00:11:39,567 --> 00:11:48,307
So now the question is, does the Venerable One remember having committed such a grave offence while involving defeat?

115
00:11:49,029 --> 00:11:49,871
I do not, friends.

116
00:11:50,634 --> 00:11:53,620
I do not remember having committed such and such a grave offence.

117
00:11:53,981 --> 00:11:57,711
In this case, the removal of litigation on account of memory should be pronounced.

118
00:11:57,943 --> 00:12:20,134
So in other words, this looks as though the witnesses, or there were no witnesses, it may just have been hearsay, and we have to give the benefit of the doubt to the Bhikkhu here who says that he doesn't remember that at all, that somebody's defaming him.

119
00:12:20,154 --> 00:12:25,621
Now, the next one is by litigation on account of past insanity.

120
00:12:27,475 --> 00:12:32,461
So here a Bhikkhu reproves another Bhikkhu of such a grave offense involving this defeat.

121
00:12:33,081 --> 00:12:37,586
Does the Venerable One remember having committed such as such a grave offense involving defeat?

122
00:12:38,167 --> 00:12:39,828
I do not remember having done so.

123
00:12:40,769 --> 00:12:44,514
Despite the denial, the former presses the latter further.

124
00:12:44,674 --> 00:12:50,181
Surely the Venerable One must know quite well if he remembers having committed such and such an offense.

125
00:12:52,123 --> 00:12:53,043
I had been out of,

126
00:12:53,176 --> 00:12:56,519
and he says, oh, I had gone mad, friend.

127
00:12:56,559 --> 00:12:57,660
I was out of my mind.

128
00:12:58,140 --> 00:13:04,368
And when I was mad, I said and did many things improper for a recluse, but I do not remember.

129
00:13:04,888 --> 00:13:06,529
I was mad when I did it.

130
00:13:08,451 --> 00:13:14,899
So in this case, because he's mad, the litigation is removed.

131
00:13:14,999 --> 00:13:17,682
It's not taken any further.

132
00:13:18,943 --> 00:13:44,866
he might of course be lying but some of you might have read a book called the man who mistook his wife for a hat which was quite a bestseller oh i don't know 80s or something it was written by a man called oliver sachs who is a great psychiatrist writer writer about about mental dis-eases and there was a case in there of a man who murdered and uh

133
00:13:45,504 --> 00:13:50,289
past mutilated is far too strong a word, but really did a lot of damage to his daughter.

134
00:13:51,191 --> 00:13:56,677
And if I remember rightly, it was an adopted daughter.

135
00:13:58,519 --> 00:14:04,705
And he couldn't remember, and he was believed that he couldn't remember, and he was put into a mental home.

136
00:14:05,346 --> 00:14:13,875
And while he was in the mental home, the nurses and people around him became more and more confident about him, and they would allow him out on a bicycle ride.

137
00:14:14,682 --> 00:14:21,190
So one day while he's riding a bicycle, he hits something and he bumps his head and he remembers what he did.

138
00:14:22,250 --> 00:14:31,361
And according to Oliver Sacks, it took them six months to stop him from trying to commit suicide.

139
00:14:32,643 --> 00:14:35,687
So things can happen where you just basically forget.

140
00:14:35,706 --> 00:14:39,211
Anyway, that's called madness.

141
00:14:41,313 --> 00:14:43,355
And as we've seen it, it probably is.

142
00:14:46,322 --> 00:14:53,460
Now there's another one which says, here there is the effecting acknowledgement of an offense.

143
00:14:53,539 --> 00:14:56,628
So in this case, a monk has done something wrong.

144
00:14:57,168 --> 00:15:00,096
He accepts it and he goes and apologizes.

145
00:15:00,548 --> 00:15:03,110
He reveals it and he discloses it.

146
00:15:03,150 --> 00:15:13,783
He goes to a senior bhikkhu and after arranging his robe, et cetera, paying homage at his feet and then sitting to the side, palms together saying, venerable sir, I've committed such and such an offense and I confess it.

147
00:15:14,323 --> 00:15:16,986
And the response is, do you see what you've done?

148
00:15:17,006 --> 00:15:17,868
He says, yes, I do.

149
00:15:18,307 --> 00:15:20,389
Will you practice restraint in the future?

150
00:15:20,850 --> 00:15:22,952
I will practice restraint in the future.

151
00:15:23,394 --> 00:15:25,777
Now this is only for the lesser rules.

152
00:15:25,797 --> 00:15:28,799
And every half moon,

153
00:15:29,253 --> 00:15:33,639
the monks get together to do this confession.

154
00:15:33,820 --> 00:15:43,995
And this is the actual dialogue, where you say you've committed so many offences, you don't have to actually name them, although in some orders you do.

155
00:15:44,696 --> 00:15:47,058
And the response is, do you see what you've done?

156
00:15:47,078 --> 00:15:48,942
You say, I have, I've seen what I've done.

157
00:15:48,981 --> 00:15:50,203
Will you restrain in the future?

158
00:15:50,764 --> 00:15:53,168
I will restrain.

159
00:15:53,384 --> 00:15:56,730
Every two weeks, as it were, the slate is wiped clean.

160
00:15:57,272 --> 00:16:00,457
But remember, this is only for the lesser offences.

161
00:16:02,601 --> 00:16:05,307
And then finally, no, that's not final.

162
00:16:05,927 --> 00:16:07,431
This is second, isn't it?

163
00:16:07,451 --> 00:16:10,256
There's the pronouncement of bad character against someone.

164
00:16:10,937 --> 00:16:16,168
So here, somebody is reproved, a great offence, one involving defeat, etc.

165
00:16:16,654 --> 00:16:27,283
And again, he says, I do not remember having committed the grave offence despite the denial of former presses.

166
00:16:28,245 --> 00:16:29,326
Again, he denies it.

167
00:16:30,086 --> 00:16:33,469
And again, despite the denial of former presses again.

168
00:16:33,489 --> 00:16:38,332
And finally, he does actually admit it.

169
00:16:38,974 --> 00:16:46,240
So this reminds you of sort of libel cases, really, where somebody is trying to defend themselves against libel.

170
00:16:46,472 --> 00:16:50,500
And eventually, of course, the libel wasn't libel at all, but it was true.

171
00:16:51,982 --> 00:16:58,374
And, of course, such a person, they have a pronouncement of bad character against someone.

172
00:16:59,316 --> 00:17:08,311
So if the order does that, I'm not exactly sure as to what that means, but probably...

173
00:17:09,912 --> 00:17:15,944
He says, I remember having committed such a grave offense, one involving defeat or voting on defeat.

174
00:17:16,025 --> 00:17:16,586
I was hurried.

175
00:17:16,625 --> 00:17:17,448
I was confused.

176
00:17:17,848 --> 00:17:26,507
When I said that, I did not remember having committed such and such a grave offense, one involving such a thing, such a pronouncement of bad character against someone.

177
00:17:27,028 --> 00:17:29,153
And so there comes a settlement on this.

178
00:17:29,487 --> 00:17:34,980
And I think once somebody knows about character, he has to leave that monastery probably.

179
00:17:35,582 --> 00:17:40,272
And once he's done that, he's got to find some other support from somewhere.

180
00:17:40,692 --> 00:17:53,048
If the lay people begin to recognise that he's not behaving properly as a monastic, they can refuse to offer him food, etc.

181
00:17:53,430 --> 00:17:57,055
Eventually, he could be driven just to disrobe.

182
00:17:57,596 --> 00:18:01,141
Now, tomorrow, we'll actually look at that type of dispute.

183
00:18:01,922 --> 00:18:04,065
But for the present time, we can leave that.

184
00:18:04,625 --> 00:18:07,189
And the final one, I think, is a lovely way to...

185
00:18:08,333 --> 00:18:35,708
to deal with something it's called covering over with grass it's not the same as sweeping under the carpet right trying to forget it so here there's quarreling and brawling deep disputes uh that they may have said and done many improper things and so they should all meet together and all meet together and and and they they try to come to some sort of um

186
00:18:37,055 --> 00:18:38,316
agreement about things.

187
00:18:38,757 --> 00:18:41,940
But the disputes and the brawling are far too much.

188
00:18:43,201 --> 00:18:47,566
And for the good of these benevolence for their own, I shall confess.

189
00:18:48,126 --> 00:18:54,054
So there's an inability for them to come to some sort of an agreement.

190
00:18:54,534 --> 00:19:00,240
And so the thing is to put grass over it and let it simmer for a while.

191
00:19:00,259 --> 00:19:01,662
Let it just rest for a while.

192
00:19:02,041 --> 00:19:06,386
When the heat's gone out of the argument, then it's possible to bring

193
00:19:09,083 --> 00:19:18,873
So those are the seven ways in which you can deal with disputes and with people who have not behaved well.

194
00:19:18,893 --> 00:19:27,240
And as I say, most of them, I think all of them actually, can be related to ordinary daily life and what happens in ordinary society.

195
00:19:27,961 --> 00:19:35,849
So this is the Buddha trying to prepare the ground for his passing.

196
00:19:36,538 --> 00:19:40,084
The Nigantha was an elder contemporary.

197
00:19:40,284 --> 00:19:48,855
I don't know quite how much older to the Buddha he was, but obviously this has now come up for really dealing with.

198
00:19:48,875 --> 00:19:58,647
And then he goes, finally, he says these six memorable qualities that create love and respect and conduce to helpfulness, to non-dispute, to non-concord and to unity.

199
00:19:58,949 --> 00:20:03,474
Now, this is repeated on another discourse that we'll be doing tomorrow.

200
00:20:03,895 --> 00:20:11,406
And we'll be looking at the actual dispute that arises, gives us some idea as to what monks can get up to.

201
00:20:13,349 --> 00:20:25,547
So I hope that at least it's been informative and has increased your confidence in the Buddha and his teachings and that this will drive you on.

202
00:20:25,914 --> 00:20:32,723
to liberate yourself from all suffering sooner rather than later.

203
00:20:32,743 --> 00:20:33,748
